Concrete findings

F1 / fig-74 / source-grid provenance and undeclared edge regularization
References: fig-74.spec.json:20, :47, :1302, :1318, :11192.
The source's left outer border is less than a full half-cell before x=-1.
The independently counted complete lattice is x=-1 through 4, ten
half-unit columns, plus a cropped left strip. In the native image the
left-border-to--1 distance is roughly 56 pixels, compared with roughly
70-73 pixels between the following half-unit lattice lines. This supports
a partial crop, not a reliable exact x=-1.5 source coordinate.
The draft instead asserts "11 complete half-unit columns" and uses a
full -1.5-to--1 cell. Its curve inventory additionally claims the same
source-frame bounds and "no source-domain extension"; sample paths
actually begin at x=-1.5. These are stronger source claims than the
pixels support.
All ten reliable source columns and all nineteen rows ARE still present;
the defect is not deletion of a full instructional cell. It is treating
an unverified edge completion as exact source data and failing to declare
the regularization. Keep complete-lattice counts/source facts separate
from the chosen modern frame, as already done for 75/76. Either retain a
partial edge strip, or explicitly document any justified modern completion
without asserting that the source supplied eleven complete columns or
that the resulting edge coordinate is an exact original datum.
No convenient exact original border coordinate is proposed by this review.

F2 / fig-75 / horizontal ruled strokes stop before both partial borders
References: fig-75.spec.json:923 (grid), :939 (border), :7692 (dimensions).
The source has ten complete columns x=-2.5..2.5 and nineteen rows
y=-0.5..9. Every original horizontal lattice line visibly continues
through the cropped side strips to the outer border.
The declared modern borders +/-2.75 are legitimate disclosed margin
choices, NOT exact source coordinates. However the candidate grid's
horizontal strokes run only from x=-2.5 to 2.5. At 640px this is
61.935..578.065px, whereas the outer frame is 36.129..603.871px.
The preview shows two blank ruled-plane strips about 25.806px wide.
Seventeen internal non-axis horizontal lines y=0.5,1,...,8.5 stop short
on each side; the x-axis and top/bottom frame still span the strips.
The full ten-by-nineteen instructional lattice passes; source continuation
across the partial cells does not. Extending those horizontal strokes to
the disclosed borders restores the source structure WITHOUT inventing
an extra complete column, moving a tick, or changing either base.
Do not "repair" this by calling the partial strips full half-unit cells.

F3 / fig-76 / horizontal ruled strokes stop before the partial left border
References: fig-76.spec.json:1220 (grid), :1236 (border), :11042 (dimensions).
The complete source lattice is ten columns x=-3..2, nineteen rows
y=-0.5..9, plus a cropped left strip. Source horizontal lattice strokes
continue through that strip to the border.
The modern left frame x=-3.4 is correctly disclosed as a regularized
addition rather than source-exact data. The grid nevertheless begins at
x=-3, leaving the left strip unruled. SVG horizontal spans begin at
78.111px, with the left border at 36.495px: a visible 41.615px blank strip
at 640px. Seventeen internal non-axis horizontal lines y=0.5..8.5
have lost their partial-strip continuation; the right x=2 border is aligned.
All complete cells and the original curve/frame arrangement remain;
repair only the missing horizontal continuations in the partial strip.

F4 / common source element / positive-axis arrowheads omitted
References: fig-74.spec.json:1354/:1416;
fig-75.spec.json:975/:1037; fig-76.spec.json:1272/:1334.
All three originals visibly include positive x/y arrowheads. All three
current previews terminate the axes as plain lines at their frame ends;
SVG inspection finds the plain axis lines and no arrow markers.
This is an actual omitted original mathematical element, not a cultural
decoration or an inferred answer. Restore deterministic positive-axis
arrowheads at the appropriate frame ends. Current tick signs and x/y names
still make the orientation readable; this finding does NOT invalidate
the independent exponential calculations or imply a reversed axis.

Per-figure substantive verdicts

fig-74: REVISE (F1 and shared F4)
- PASS: a single shared frame; all three supplied bases; curve labels
  associated with the correct steep/intermediate/shallow branches;
  positive increasing curves; correct order reversal across x=0;
  common (0,1), distinguished from origin O at (0,0).
- PASS: every sample coordinate equals the labeled base^x to the local
  numeric precision; every curve explicitly contains hidden sample (0,1).
  No new visible point marker; strokes remain solid. The 3^x graph
  preserves the instructional queries of 628 and comparison of 630.
- Current SVG has twelve vertical grid strokes (eleven full columns)
  and twenty horizontal strokes (nineteen rows), half-unit spacing.
  All reliable complete source lattice is included; F1 concerns the
  extra complete left cell being misrepresented as source-exact.
- Curve labels are horizontal clean typesetting, with no wrong base,
  curve-label collision or missing numeric sign seen at 640px.
- y=1 tick: actual grid/tick height 863.738px; numeral center 839.738px,
  a 24px upward displacement. It is visibly offset rather than aligned.
  The coordinate tick and common point stay at y=1, and the numeral does
  not land on the y=1.5 grid line (812.944px). No actual swapped tick,
  changed common-point ordinate or misassociated curve was observed.
  Do not add a defect solely from the known displacement.
- No unwanted viewport clipping. Steep branches stop at intended y=9
  frame intersections; shallow branch reaches the frame. No answer
  annotation, Cyrillic/Chinese visible label, cultural symbol or texture.

fig-75: REVISE (F2 and shared F4)
- PASS: ten complete half-unit columns, nineteen rows; correct common
  frame, supplied bases (1/2) and 2; reflection in the y-axis;
  common (0,1), not origin (0,0).
- Geometry samples satisfy their labeled exponentials; both include
  (0,1), all visible curves solid. No gratuitous visible point marker.
  Decreasing-curve reads and strict inequality directions for 633 match
  the frozen solution. The template's +1.5 is retained.
- Modern +/-2.75 borders are disclosed additions and preserve the
  symmetric frame; no complete source lattice interval is removed.
  The missing partial-strip ruling in F2 remains a real structural issue.
- Labels sit beside the correct upper-left and upper-right branches.
  y=1 numeral is at the correct grid height 877.645px with a larger
  horizontal clearance. No misleading shift in ordinate.
- No culture/answer leakage or label clipping at 640px. The entire border
  and all queried graph regions are visible. Frame-edge curve clipping
  is intentional and compatible with the disclosed modern margins.

fig-76: REVISE (F3 and shared F4)
- PASS: all ten complete half-unit columns, nineteen rows; one shared
  frame with bases 1/3,0.25,0.8; common (0,1), distinct origin.
- PASS: all samples equal the labeled base^x. At x=-1 the ideal heights
  are 3,4,1.25; at x=1 they are 1/3,0.25,0.8. Left-side/right-side
  curve order is correct, all decreasing and positive.
- Modern -3.4 left frame is a disclosed partial-margin choice, not an
  exact original abscissa. F3 affects partial-strip horizontal ruling,
  NOT deletion of any full half-unit column or row.
- Upper-left 1/3 label, upper-central/right 0.25 label and lower-left
  shallow 0.8 label have correct association and readable clearance.
- y=1 tick: actual grid/tick height 884.327px, numeral center 908.327px,
  a 24px downward displacement. It does not coincide with the y=0.5
  grid line (936.346px), and no new ordinate/common-point claim is made.
  The source numeral was also displaced below this line. No actual
  association error was observed; known offset alone is not a defect.
- No visible cultural or answer leakage, missing label, unintended label
  clipping or blank raster. Steep branches meet the y=9 frame; the
  shallow branch reaches both frame sides as intended.

fig-77 source-only verdict: SOURCE_READING_HOLD FOR EXACT BASES
- Original labels are Roman I, II, III ONLY; no numeric base labels may
  be fabricated. No spec/output for 77 inspected or constructed.
- Exercise 636 gives y=a^x, so the exact valid method is a=y(1).
  Source-only coarse readings from the frozen first stage: I about 0.6,
  II about 2.5, III roughly 1.8-2, with the recorded uncertainty bands.
  These are approximate graph readings, not reliable exact fractions
  or exact decimal bases, and not calibrated grading tolerances.
- Reliable inequalities: 0<a_I<1 and 1<a_III<a_II.
- No additional exact base evidence found within the authorized scope.
  Do not choose convenient bases or infer original equation labels.
  Keep only the affected exact-base/77 construction stage on HOLD.

